.TH MU2EDAQ-GENERATE-DOCS 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
generate-docs.py \- regenerate the generated man page sections, and check the
documentation against the code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./venv/bin/python tools/generate-docs.py
.RB [ \-\-check | \-\-write ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The check reference in
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery (7)
is a transcription of the live check registry, and transcriptions rot: a check
added to the package is registered, selectable in
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5)
and reported on, and nothing otherwise forces anyone to mention it in the
manual. That table was first produced by a throwaway script that was not kept,
so there was no way to tell whether the page still matched the code. This is
that script, kept, and wired into the build and the test suite.
.PP
It is run three ways: by hand, as the
.B docs
build target
.RB ( \-\-write ),
and as the
.B docs-check
.BR ctest (1)
entry and
.I tests/unit/test_docs.py
.RB ( \-\-check ).
.SH CHECKS
Four things are verified. Only the first is repaired.
.TP
.B 1. the generated check reference
The
.B CHECK REFERENCE
block of
.I man/man7/mu2edaq-power-recovery.7
against the live registry, its descriptions and its root requirements. The
block is delimited by troff comments so the surrounding prose stays
hand-written, and
.B \-\-write
rewrites what lies between them. It is emitted as
.B .TP
entries rather than a
.BR tbl (1)
table deliberately: tbl needs a preprocessor pass, and a page with an
unpreprocessed
.B .TS
block renders as garbage under plain
.BR "groff \-man" ,
which is how these pages get read during a recovery.
.TP
.B 2. a man page for every console script
Every name under
.I [project.scripts]
in
.I pyproject.toml
must have a
.IR man/man1/ \fIname\fR .1
page. Extra man1 pages are not flagged: several things here are documented but
are not console scripts \(em
.BR mu2e-probe (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery-bootstrap (1),
and this page.
.TP
.B 3. a man 5 entry for every configuration key
Every dotted key in
.I settings.DEFAULTS
must be named in some
.I man/man5
page. Read
.B SCOPE OF CHECK 3
below before relying on this.
.TP
.B 4. no groff warnings
Every page under
.I man/
is rendered with
.B "groff \-man \-Tascii \-ww"
and any diagnostic on standard error is a failure. A groff warning is not
cosmetic: the usual causes \(em an unescaped leading
.RB ' . '
inside an
.B .EX
block, an unknown macro, an unterminated font change \(em silently drop or
mangle the text someone is reading in the middle of an outage. When
.BR groff (1)
is not installed the check is skipped, with a note.
.SH SCOPE OF CHECK 3
Check 3 is
.I section-scoped identifier matching,
not dotted-path matching, and the difference is worth knowing before quoting it
as a guarantee.
.PP
A key such as
.I vault.addr
is split at the first dot. The page is searched for a
.B .SS vault
section, and each remaining component \(em here
.I addr
\(em must appear as an argument to a font macro
.RB ( .B ", " .I ", " .BR
and so on) somewhere inside it. The man pages are written that way on purpose:
.B .SS vault
followed by
.BR ".I addr" ,
rather than repeating the full path on every entry. Only 12 of the 77 keys have
their complete dotted path written out verbatim anywhere in
.IR man/man5 .
.PP
So the check will not catch a leaf documented under the wrong parent, and a leaf
whose name collides with an unrelated identifier named in the same section would
pass without being documented. It was checked by hand against the current pages
and no such false pass exists, but the guarantee is
.I "every key's name appears in the right section,"
not
.I "every key is documented."
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT CHECK
.IP \(bu 2
.B Itself.
Check 2 iterates
.I [project.scripts]
only, so a tool under
.I tools/
is structurally invisible to it. This page exists because that gap left
.I generate-docs.py
undocumented.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Prose against behaviour.
Nothing here reads a sentence in a man page or in
.I README.md
and compares it with what the code does. Every wrong safety claim this project
has had passed all four checks.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Markdown.
.IR README.md ", " CLAUDE.md ", " PROJECT-STATUS.md
and
.I docs/
are not examined at all.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report drift and exit non-zero; change nothing. This is the default, and it is
what
.B ctest
and the test suite run.
.TP
.B \-\-write
Regenerate the generated sections in place. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-check .
.TP
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
Project root. Defaults to the tree this script is in.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing on success.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No problems. Unless
.B \-q
was given, a one-line summary is printed: the number of man pages, console
scripts and configuration keys covered.
.TP
.B 1
At least one problem, each listed on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the documentation against the code, changing nothing:
.RS
.EX
\&./venv/bin/python tools/generate\-docs.py \-\-check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Regenerate the check reference after adding or renaming a check, then confirm
the page still renders:
.RS
.EX
\&./venv/bin/python tools/generate\-docs.py \-\-write
groff \-man \-Tascii \-ww man/man7/mu2edaq\-power\-recovery.7 >/dev/null
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same, through the build tree:
.RS
.EX
cmake \-\-build build \-\-target docs
ctest \-\-test\-dir build \-R docs\-check \-\-output\-on\-failure
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I tools/generate-docs.py
The script.
.TP
.I man/man7/mu2edaq-power-recovery.7
The only page it writes to, and only between its generated-block markers.
.TP
.I tests/unit/test_docs.py
Runs
.B \-\-check
as part of
.BR pytest (1),
and asserts that
.B \-\-check
writes nothing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery (7),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery.yaml (5),
.BR groff (1),
.BR ctest (1)
